$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\le \\frac{x - 1}{6} \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le x - 1 \\le 6 \\\\ -5 & \\le x \\le 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186068491 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\dfrac{x-1}{6}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $1.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\dfrac{x-1}{6} \\leq  1\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\frac{x-1}{6}\\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le x-1\\le 6 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068493 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\mid 12x - 5 \\mid \\ge 7$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186068493 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|12x - 5| > 7.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068493 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value.\n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12x - 5$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $7$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n12x - 5\\begin{vmatrix}\\le - 7 & or & 12x - 5\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 7\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 5 & \\le - 7 & 12x - 5 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 12x & \\le - 2 & 12x & \\ge 12 \\\\ x & \\le - \\frac{2}{12} & x & \\ge \\frac{12}{12} \\\\ x & \\le - \\frac{1}{6} & x & \\ge 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\nx\\begin{vmatrix}\\le - \\frac{1}{6} & or & x\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 1\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186068493 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12x - 5$ whose distance from zero is greater than $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 & \\text{or} &  & 12x-5 & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 &  &  & 12x-5 & >7 \\\\ 12x & <-2 &  &  & 12x & >12 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{2}{12} &  &  & x & >\\frac{12}{12} \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{6} &  &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-\\frac{1}{6} & \\text{or} &  & x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068510 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|10-2x|+1 & <5 \\\\ |10-2x| & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $10-2x$ whose distance from zero is less than $4.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 < 10 - 2x < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4<10-2x & <4 \\\\ -14<-2x & <-6 \\\\ 14>2x & >6 \\\\ 7>x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3 < x < 7\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068510 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}10 - 2x & + 1\\end{vmatrix}< 5; \\mid 10 - 2x \\mid \\mid < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value.\n\nThe inequality specifies every number $10-2x$ whose distance from zero is less than $4$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 < 10 - 2x < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 < 10 - 2x & < 4 \\\\ -14 < - 2x & < - 6 \\\\ 14 > 2x & > 6 \\\\ 7 > x & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3 < x < 7\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186068717 1038 17592186511567 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068717 1038 17592186511580 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068718 1038 17592186511521 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068719 1038 17592186511463 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068719 1038 17592186511490 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068719 1038 17592186511573 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186068978 1050 780.0 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069133 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069133 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $1 + \\mid 17 - 3h \\mid \\le 9$. {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n$h \\le$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186069133 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $1+|17-3h|\\le 9.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$h\\le $\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069133 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+|17-3h| & \\le 9 \\\\ |17-3h| & \\le 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $17-3h$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $8.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-8\\le 17-3h\\le 8\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8\\le 17-3h & \\le 8 \\\\ -25\\le -3h & \\le -9 \\\\ 25\\ge 3h & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{25}{3}\\ge h & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3\\le h\\le \\frac{25}{3}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069135 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069135 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\mid \\frac{5 - 9a}{2} \\mid \\ge 7$.$a$\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nor $a \\ge$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186069135 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{5-9a}{2}|\\ge 7.$\n\n$a$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ or $\\,a\\ge $\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186069135 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{5-9a}{2}$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 & \\text{or} &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 &  &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7 \\\\ 5-9a & \\le -14 &  &  & 5-9a & \\ge 14 \\\\ -9a & \\le -19 &  &  & -9a & \\ge 9 \\\\ 9a & \\ge 19 &  &  & 9a & \\le -9 \\\\ a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9} &  &  & a & \\le -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186070026 1064 "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\mid 2x+3 \\mid \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n-5 \\le 2x + 3 \\le 5; - 8 \\le 2x \\le 2; - 4 \\le x \\le 1\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186070026 1064 "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n|2x+3| \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5.$ So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\le 2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -8\\le 2x\\le 2 \\\\ -4\\le x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186070027 1064 "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6$. This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx\\begin{vmatrix}\\le - 6 & or & x\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 6\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities\n\n$$\nx\\leq-6\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx\\geq 6\n$$\n\nneeds to be true for\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid \\geq 6\n$$\n\nto be true." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186070027 1064 "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n|x| \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -6 & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities $x\\leq-6$ or $x\\geq 6$ needs to be true for $|x| \\geq 6$ to be true." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186092181 1038 17592186511543 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186092388 1038 17592186511500 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186092391 1038 17592186511513 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186098013 1078 17592186098016 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186098295 1078 17592186098300 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [1759218609902