tive of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186100581 1071 "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186100581 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3)$, and\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\non this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 3 \\\\\nf(3) &= f(-2) + \\int_{-2}^{3}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 3 + (-10) \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=3\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=-2\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186100581 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and $f'(x)\\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+(-10) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186100582 1071 "![](images/b2/b2f034132460f2a6b3a26eb52bd4129fb13ea5a871d766815b83047217b97b3b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8$. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(-2)=4$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186100582 1071 "![](images/b2/b2f034132460f2a6b3a26eb52bd4129fb13ea5a871d766815b83047217b97b3b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186100582 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-2,5)$, and\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\neq 0\n$$\n\non this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 4 \\\\\nf(5) &= f(-2) + \\int_{-2}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{4, -4\\} = -4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = -4\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_{\\text{min}} = 5\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186100582 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-2,5),$ and $f'(x) \\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{4, -4\\} = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = -4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_{\\text{min}} = 5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186100618 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1$?\n${\\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 1 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x =-1$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186100618 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n![](images/ed/ed102b504e05beeadc9c50229066f14d39ff9f39730f122a40d3597861755486.svg)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186100618 1074 "First, we factor t