lator is required to answer this question." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101113 1071 "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300.$ The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2\\,000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101113 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101160 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101160 1071 "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 2, 2]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 2, 2]$ given that $f(-2) = 2$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101160 1071 "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101160 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,9\\}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101284 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101284 1071 "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 3, 3]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ given that $f(-3) =-6$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101284 1071 "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-3)=-6.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101284 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101440 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101440 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x =-2$?\n$g(x) = {\\frac{x^{2} + 5x + 6}{kx + 2k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 2 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x =-2$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101440 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k}, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ k, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101440 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x+2)}{k(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{k\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=-2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{x+3}{k})=\\frac{1}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=\\pm 1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101475 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101475 1071 "![](images/d6/d652e7ffe301986acf3b5ed02c00cc775d5f9ec2e7849248b7478e90a2a4aacb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 2, 2]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 2, 2]$ given that $f(-2) =-5$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101475 1071 "![](images/d6/d652e7ffe301986acf3b5ed02c00cc775d5f9ec2e7849248b7478e90a2a4aacb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=-5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101475 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1+5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-5,-6,-1\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101504 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101504 1071 "A factory spends $C(x) = x^{4} + 13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101504 1071 "A factory spends $C(x)={x}^{4}+13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121\\,000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101504 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=121\\cdot x=121x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =121x-({x}^{4}+13x) \\\\  & =108x-{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=4$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<4.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=108-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 108-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=3$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(3)=-12(3)^{2}=-108<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that $x=3$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=3$ and $x=4.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =108(0)-{0}^{4}=0 \\\\ P(3) & =108(3)-{3}^{4}=243\\,✓ \\\\ P(4) & =108(4)-{4}^{4}=176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $$243\\,000.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101564 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101564 1071 "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200$. The cost of providing the course is $C(x) = 200x - 4x^{3} + 0.3x^{4}$, where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101564 1071 "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200.$ The cost of providing the course is $C(x)=200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4},$ where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101564 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates' test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ participants, the computer science center has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-0.3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $3\\le x\\le 13.$ Therefore, $x=3$ and $x=13$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $3\\ge x\\ge 13.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 1.2{x}^{2}(10-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ However, the values $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=10.$\n\nLet's check if $x=10$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =24x-3.6{x}^{2} \\\\ {P}^{″}(10) & =240-360=-120<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at  $x=10$ is negative, the point $x=10$ is a maximum. \n\nOur candidates are $x=3,x=10$ and $x=13.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =4(3)^{3}-0.3(3)^{4}=83.7 \\\\ P(10) & =4(10)^{3}-0.3(10)^{4}=1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ P(13) & =4(13)^{3}-0.3(13)^{4}=219.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum profit is $$1\\,000.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101902 1061 606252 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101902 1061 644177 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101903 1061 606253 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101903 1061 644178 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101904 1061 606256 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101904 1061 644181 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101905 1061 606257 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101905 1061 644182 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101906 1061 606260 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101906 1061 644186 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101907 1061 606266 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101907 1061 644190 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101994 1064 "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good.\n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= (fixed cost) + x \\cdot (cost per unit)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company.\n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x)$, then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x)\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101994 1064 "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good. \n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =(\\text{fixed cost})+x\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company. \n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x),$ then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x).\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186101995 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(1)=k\n$$\n\n, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186101995 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/cb/cbdbe44a94ff036c74957691e30ed3652d50d2a8dbaa50b29827408f242492bb.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=k.$ So, $f(1)=k$, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1,$ we need to choose $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186102014 1064 "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. We can solve these problems using the second derivative or the candidates test. Let's find out how." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186102014 1064 "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. The candidates test gives them a method for doing this. Let's find out how." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186102025 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to maximize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x)$, the *derivative* of $f(x)$, is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0f93de1825106880b3fdcd3c704c72e0748df277e5098064d6ee9abc9bdf8.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that\n\n$$\nf(-2)=3\n$$\n\nOur goal is find the *maximum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2]$.\n\nTo find the maximum value of $f(x)$, we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on 