3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =(8+10+7+9)+(-16-6+5-12) \\\\  & =34-29 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134377 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134377 1071 "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t) = ⟨e^{\\cos t}, e^{\\sin t}⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π]$. The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$Area(D) = ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}AAAAAAAdt$,\n\nwhere $P(x, y) = 0$ and $Q(x, y) = x$. Determine the missing part of the equation." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186134377 1071 "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134377 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134471 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134471 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{2} + 4$. For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x) = k$ has a solution on the interval $x ∈ [2, 5]$?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186134471 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [2,5]?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134471 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,5],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(5).$\n\nSince $f(2)=8$ and $f(5)=29,$ we must have \n$k\\in [8,29].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=10$ belongs to the interval $[8,29].$\n\n![](images/14/14acd67d0b773864d39aaafcc6b41c9d8f3f10317b5e3f4584711baf157f346d.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134472 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134472 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x - 1}$?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186134472 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134472 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,2]$ and $[-1,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134476 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134476 1071 "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III listed below be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\nI. increasing\n\nII. continuous\n\nIII. $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134476 1071 "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n> *Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $______$ on a closed interval $[a, b]$. If $k$ is a number that lies between $_________$, then there exists a number $c ∈ [a, b]$ such that $f(c) = k$.*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III listed below be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\n1. increasing\n2. continuous\n3. $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186134476 1074 "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III\"." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134541 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134541 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/2|}}{4},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186134541 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = \\frac{e^{-\\mid x/2 \\mid}}{4}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$,\nits cumulative distribution function is\n$F(x) = {\\frac{e^{x/2}}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ h(x),\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0$.\nFind the function $h(x)$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186134541 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186135022 1064 "The **intermediate value theorem (IVT)** states that:\n\n*If $f(x)$ is continuous on $\\color{blue}[a,b],$ and $k$ is a number between $\\color{red}{f(a)}$ and $\\color{red}{f(b)}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in \\color{blue}{[a,b]}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nThe intuition behind the IVT is that if a function is continuous between two inputs, then between those two inputs, we must be able to draw the function without picking up our pen off the page, so the function must cover all values between the two corresponding outputs.\n\nThe IVT is often used to prove that some equation has a solution. For example, if we want to prove that there is a solution to $f(x)=3,$ and we know that $f(x)$ is a continuous function with $f({\\color{blue}{1}})={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $f({\\color{blue}{6}})={\\color{red}{6}},$ then the IVT tells us that there exists some solution $c \\in \\color{blue}{[1,6]}$ such that $f(c)=3.$\n\nWe can gain further intuition by visualizing the situation. First, let's plot what $y=f(x)$ *might* look like.\n\n![](images/98/9896ea1c3d3d7c461e78ce4b4b232ce1f13f329468cd28b2d8e743132de093df.png)\n\nFor this particular $f(x)$ there is one solution to $f(x)=3$, which is $x\\approx 2.$ Note that our solution lies inside the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\nNow let's consider another *possible* $f(x).$\n\n![](images/de/dea5683d5b18bccf9623fb6a107e8ef818ddd1cef4adbef08be471869791ec27.png)\n\nThis time, there are three solutions to $f(x)=3$ which lie in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$ \n\nWe can keep changing our $f(x),$ but no matter how we draw it, we 