032ef0726fec.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186135023 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+5, & 1\\le x<3 \\\\ -x+4, & 3\\le x<4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186135023 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n![](images/0d/0d18168108342ef8232063b58e46ef88cdff9fe54aa730dca872bc9755a42995.svg)\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1075 17592186155668 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186155667 1075 17592186155669 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186155667 1075 17592186155670 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186155667 1075 17592186155671 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186155667 1075 17592186155672 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186155667 1075 17592186510837 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1075 17592186510932 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1075 17592186511027 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1075 17592186511038 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1075 17592186511095 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186155667 1078 17592186511038 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157273 1038 17592186511559 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 17592186511527 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157424 1038 17592186511470 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157425 1038 17592186511458 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157425 1038 17592186511486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157425 1038 17592186511545 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157791 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157791 1071 "Find $f^{″}(x, y)$ for the function $f(x, y)$ given that\n$f^{′}(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - y & -x\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186157791 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186157791 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186159470 1078 17592186159473 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162014 1038 17592186511494 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162015 1038 17592186511495 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162041 1038 17592186511512 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162089 1038 17592186511514 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162091 1038 17592186511568 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162110 1038 17592186511544 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162378 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162378 1071 "Given that $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{50}∼N(5, 10^{2})$ is a random sample of size $50$ from a normal population, calculate $P(4.8 < X^(―) < 5.2)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.1$ | $0.5398$ | $0.5438$ | $0.5478$ | $0.5517$ | $0.5557$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.5793$ | $0.5832$ | $0.5871$ | $0.5910$ | $0.5948$ |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6179$ | $0.6217$ | $0.6255$ | $0.6293$ | $0.6331$ |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6554$ | $0.6591$ | $0.6628$ | $0.6664$ | $0.6700$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186162378 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}∼N(5,{10}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $50$ from a normal population, calculate $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186162378 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{10}^{2}}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =P(\\frac{4.8-5}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{5.2-5}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.14<Z<0.14) \\\\  & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,Φ(-0.14) & =1-Φ(0.14) \\\\  & =1-0.5557 \\\\  & =0.4443.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14) \\\\  & =0.5557-0.4443 \\\\  & =0.1114.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186164979 1075 17592186511023 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186164979 1078 17592186511023 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186166961 1038 17592186511532 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186167293 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186167293 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186167293 1071 "$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x = π$.The Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x = π$." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186167293 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{1+0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186172089 1038 17592186511492 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186172090 1038 17592186511541 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186172090 1038 17592186511546 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186186402 1078 17592186186403 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188328 1038 17592186511491 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188328 1038 17592186511582 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188339 1038 17592186511456 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188339 1038 17592186511574 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188687 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188687 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 1\\end{bmatrix}$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 1} = \\frac{6}{5}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535523 17592186045417 false] [17592186188687 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186188687 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}]-{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{36}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192701 1038 17592186511579 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192702 1038 17592186511539 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192702 1038 17592186511563 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192712 1038 17592186511455 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192712 1038 17592186511578 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186192742 1038 17592186511550 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186199502 1064 "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(