$ |" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511453 1086 #uuid "1f7f0079-ead3-5b48-9ed7-038c428c6b95" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511453 1087 17592186511590 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511610 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511637 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511650 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511664 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511669 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1084 17592186511671 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1085 "![](images/23/2364a0b3b1f379c1b091528ffeed46caafad8db153d63979e36ab9cc6b0a6c9d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $O$ is the center of the circle, $∠AOD$ and $∠BOE$ are straight angles, $m∠BOC={(5x+5)}^{∘},$ and $m∠DOE={(5x-5)}^{∘}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1086 #uuid "2da1acf2-080b-52d3-9b85-82ed9d0b8cb2" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511454 1087 17592186511664 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1068 17592186511335 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1069 "q-346315" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1071 "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[5,20].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1072 #uuid "0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511455 1074 "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{20-5}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+4+1+3+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =39\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1068 17592186511384 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1069 "q-146226" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=0]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|0}=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1072 #uuid "047bb67d-8c03-57ec-a81f-c32911be6893" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511456 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{16}{27}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{11}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{11}$ | $\\frac{7}{11}$ | $\\frac{3}{11}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=0] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)]-{\\mu }_{Y|0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{16}+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}+{8}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}]-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =0+6+4-4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1068 17592186511426 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1069 "q-42239" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1071 "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1072 #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511457 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1068 17592186511376 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1069 "q-80943" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1072 #uuid "0614088a-4b37-5f45-a256-f7dd1a417f0a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511458 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1068 17592186511412 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1069 "q-343672" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1071 "![](images/c5/c5f0d970e95b7e2e2656ef579f9d5be49eb9eddb433a5921149347ef349139c5.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $6$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $g(-3)=2.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1072 #uuid "065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511459 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =2 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1068 17592186511355 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1069 "q-105709" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1072 #uuid "0a118e6f-2768-508a-b130-7e64bda76208" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511460 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-4x+4=0,$ which corresponds to $x=2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,1]$ is allowed. The intervals $(-2,1]$ and $[-1,2)$ are not closed, while $[0,2]$ and $[-1,3]$ contain the point $x=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1068 17592186511442 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1069 "q-48751" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-3?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k}, & x<-3 \\\\ -\\frac{kx}{3}, & x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1072 #uuid "0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511461 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1068 17592186511310 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1069 "q-107281" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1072 #uuid "0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511462 1074 "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+4)}{(x+4)(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne -4,1,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=1.$ So statement I is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x),$ and  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $1$ | $1.001$ | $1.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-99$ | $-999$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 1$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 1$ from the right. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement II is false and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3de7d0a26f6f171fc83bd885eed9ff9f50bddfc76b2f58e9477241fb874c9b7d.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1068 17592186511431 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1069 "q-19350" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|5x+2|\\ge 1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1072 #uuid "0f90e4de-4957-59b8-990a-09a9092a26e8" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511463 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $5x+2$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $1.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & 5x+2 & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & \\le -1 &  &  & 5x+2 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 5x & \\le -3 &  &  & 5x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{3}{5} &  &  & x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -\\frac{3}{5} & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1068 17592186511324 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1069 "q-87569" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1071 "An auto parts store sells $x$ units of a certain product at a price per unit $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $1$ and $6$ units of the product each day, how many units should the store sell to maximize its revenue?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1072 #uuid "13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511464 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+63x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+63,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+63 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+21 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=3$ and $x=7.$ However, the value $x=7$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only new candidate is $x=3.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,3$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+63(1)+11=60 \\\\ R(3) & =(3)^{3}-15(3)^{2}+63(3)+11=92\\,✓ \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-15(6)^{2}+63(6)+11=65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the auto parts store should sell $3$ units." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1068 17592186511351 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1069 "q-105670" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[-2,2]?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1072 #uuid "14a3bc41-5c72-5f24-9c07-376f58252a5e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511465 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=-4$ and $f(2)=0,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-4,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,0].$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1068 17592186511399 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1069 "q-105280" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1072 #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511466 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1068 17592186511336 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1069 "q-347034" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1071 "A private art studio can enroll between $8$ and $14$ students in a painting workshop. The fee that each student pays is $$72$ if exactly $8$ students enroll, and if more than $8$ students are enrolled, the fee drops by $$4$ for each additional student. Each student pays the same fee.\n\nHow many students must the workshop have to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize its revenue, the workshop must have\n{{field-1}}\nstudents." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1072 #uuid "1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511467 1074 "First, note that if there are $8+x$ students, then the fee is $72-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(8+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of students}}\\times {{(72-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fee}} \\\\  & =(8+x)(72-4x) \\\\  & =576-32x+72x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =576+40x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the workshop can only have between $8$ and $14$ students, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n8\\le x+8\\le 14⇒0\\le x\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=40-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40-8x & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the domain $0<x<6.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ gives a maximum. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the workshop must have $8+5=13$ students enrolled to maximize its revenue." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1068 17592186511316 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1069 "q-342847" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1071 "![](images/39/393c612a7ecb682e28bf9de46024a3681e8f6e58a9add78c0b24a870aa00fa7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,4]$ given that $f(0)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1072 #uuid "1b07da73-507b-5a7d-831e-9986becac517" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511468 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+(-8) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-9\\}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-9,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1068 17592186511375 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1069 "q-342903" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1071 "![](images/31/3123021ea5734c0ff2e8edcb716245b603de6f9dde7ebf0d367e1eeb8383bdf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $10$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,0]$ given that $f(-6)=2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1072 #uuid "20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511469 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-3,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =2 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3-10 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,5,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1068 17592186511439 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1069 "q-80946" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1071 "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,0).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1072 #uuid "223797a4-3fdc-5efb-a9f2-b733398cd415" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511470 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-1)\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=-1.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1068 17592186511319 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1069 "q-347272" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1071 "A museum sells daily entry passes. The price of each pass is $$20.$ The cost of operating the museum includes a fixed daily cost of $$1,100$ and a variable cost of $$5$ per pass sold. If the museum can sell at most $300$ passes per day, what is the number of passes that maximizes the museum's profit?\n\nThe number of passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1072 #uuid "244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511471 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ passes per day, the museum has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=20x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the museum is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{1100}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{5x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =20x-(1100+5x) \\\\  & =15x-1100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 300.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=300$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=15,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=300.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1100, \\\\ P(300) & =15(300)-1100=4500-1100=3400\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of passes that maximizes the museum’s profit is $300.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1068 17592186511407 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1069 "q-340776" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1072 #uuid "270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511472 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$  for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=4.$ Therefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4$ is ${a}_{0}+8.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1068 17592186511347 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1069 "q-105705" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-9}?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1072 #uuid "2715d4ad-4bbd-59ce-9226-02bf5ba9575d" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511473 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-9=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-8,-4]$ is allowed. The intervals $(3,4)$ and $(-7,-3)$ are not closed, the interval $[-6,-1]$ contains the point $x=-3,$ while the interval $[3,5]$ contain the point $x=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1068 17592186511346 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1069 "q-343704" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1071 "![](images/a3/a3f57d0658f89f37df9a838b4a79cb9d4ed3d4183187013923c26fb3bd836e10.png)\n\nThe function $k(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,7].$ The graph of ${k}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $k(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $7,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $k(x)$ on the interval $[-2,7]$ given that $k(-2)=2.$\n\n${k}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1072 #uuid "27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511474 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $k(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${k}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${k}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1,5.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $k$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-2) & =2 \\\\ k(1) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ k(5) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ k(7) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{7}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7-2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $k$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $k$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-2,5,3\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $k$ is ${k}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $k(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1068 17592186511389 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1069 "q-18671" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1071 "A hotel has $71$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $$50$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $$2$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance per rented room is $$4$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1072 #uuid "2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511475 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(71-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(50+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $$98$ per night." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1068 17592186511373 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1069 "q-140126" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1072 #uuid "2cc2023e-80ab-5c70-bfaa-8494aaa05a23" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511476 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot v-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =-2(3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +2((-2)\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 0+5\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & -4(3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =-2(21)+2(-5)-4(-2) \\\\  & =-42-10+8 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1068 17592186511394 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1069 "q-339609" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $b$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ b, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1072 #uuid "2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511477 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1068 17592186511387 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1069 "q-119010" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1071 "A coffee producer company estimates that the profit of its coffee production, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-0.002{x}^{2}+3.9x-3,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ are the tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily. Given that the company can produce at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, how many kilograms should the company produce per day to maximize its daily profit?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1072 #uuid "300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511478 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily, and since the company produces at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<1\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-0.004x+3.9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -0.004x+3.9 & =0 \\\\ x & =975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=975$ lies in the interval $0<x<1\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=975$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.004<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=975$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=975$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $975\\cdot 10=9\\,750$ kilograms of coffee per day to maximize its daily profit." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1068 17592186511420 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1069 "q-74190" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -1 \\\\ b \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1072 #uuid "308fc3e8-c583-59b4-999d-1160fdbc5784" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511479 1074 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 2a-4-2b+10 & =0 \\\\ 2a-2b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+b\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ a+3+b-6 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-2b=-6 \\\\ a+b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-4b=-12\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-2(3) & =-6 \\\\ 2a-6 & =-6 \\\\ 2a & =0 \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=0-3=-3.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1068 17592186511445 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1069 "q-119021" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1071 "A clothes store sells $x$ hundreds of pants at a price $p(x),$  in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)=200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $500$ and $1\\,000$ pants per month, how many pants should it sell monthly to maximize its revenue?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1072 #uuid "397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511480 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ pants, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =200x+40{x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $5\\le x\\le 10,$  since $x=5$  corresponds to $5\\cdot 100=500$  and $x=10$ corresponds to $10\\cdot 100=1\\,000.$  Therefore, $x=5$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $5<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=200+80x-10{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-80x-200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2$ and $x=10.$ However, the value $x=-2$  is not in the permitted domain. Moreover, $x=10$ is already a candidate, so our candidates are  $x=5$ and $x=10.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(5) & =200(5)+40(5)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{5}^{3}≈1\\,583 \\\\ R(10) & =200(10)+40(10)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{10}^{3}≈2\\,667\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the clothes store should sell $1\\,000$ pants monthly." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1068 17592186511380 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1069 "q-343680" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1071 "![](images/d4/d4b4735b3154603284ed6f232ae640c5f47a536a5c26b75bb99970c8d0cb2c08.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $3$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $h(-4)=-6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1072 #uuid "3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511481 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-4) & =-6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ h(2) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+(5-3) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-1,-4\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1068 17592186511364 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1069 "q-103946" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1072 #uuid "3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511482 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1068 17592186511309 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1069 "q-161775" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1072 #uuid "3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511483 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/e6/e63035cbe368a4f41b7197d70880d0639f004e26996fe6b5055e69966e0f66ee.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1068 17592186511388 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1069 "q-346940" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1071 "The manager of a bakery estimates that the profit of the bakery, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-2{x}^{2}+120x-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of loaves baked daily. Given that the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, how many loaves should the bakery bake per day to maximize its daily profit?\n\nThe bakery should bake\n{{field-1}} loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1072 #uuid "3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511484 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of loaves baked daily and the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 400.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<400.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-4x+120,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -4x+120 & =0 \\\\ x & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=30$ lies in the interval $0<x<400.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=30$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=30$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=30$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$\n\nTherefore, the bakery should bake $30$ loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1068 17592186511329 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1069 "q-343884" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1071 "![](images/01/01dad591c5f02e0528e9092a058b44806cb0bab6f709347f8f90120c58ad25bc.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ if $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1072 #uuid "457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511485 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function  ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/eb/ebcaf43d56e8854ce17d89ce66a811ad1f505f7ebb855362798d845164a80377.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1068 17592186511405 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1069 "q-80945" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1072 #uuid "46310383-5e9b-5711-9143-446211dee40a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511486 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1068 17592186511349 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1069 "q-144866" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1071 "![](images/db/dbbbcdb54dc8d6f96957ca01f4eeccea0c24c79eb95fc6c10953534d38e4d724.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-8,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6,$ $2,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-8,0]$ given that $f(-8)=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1072 #uuid "4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511487 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-8,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-8,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-8,-4,-2,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-8) & =2 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,8,6,10\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-8.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1068 17592186511342 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1069 "q-343665" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1071 "![](images/d7/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1072 #uuid "47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511488 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1068 17592186511379 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1069 "q-15742" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1071 "![](images/d8/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1072 #uuid "488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511489 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1068 17592186511443 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1069 "q-29943" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|8-2z|>2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1072 #uuid "4a81effc-bede-5f14-bdb7-3f40c0a9e067" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511490 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $8-2z$ whose distance from zero is greater than $2.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-2z & <-2 & \\text{or} &  & 8-2z & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-2z & <-2 &  &  & 8-2z & >2 \\\\ -2z & <-10 &  &  & -2z & >-6 \\\\ z & >5 &  &  & z & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z<3 &  & \\text{or} &  & z>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1068 17592186511372 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1069 "q-146278" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1072 #uuid "4b9363aa-00d2-51a2-9bff-045f9cb8f2d6" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511491 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}]-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =6+27-25 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1068 17592186511337 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1069 "q-149800" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta }$ for $1\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1072 #uuid "4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511492 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1068 17592186511406 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1069 "q-48747" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}, & x\\ne 2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1072 #uuid "4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511493 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(x-4)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2}{x-4}=\\frac{2+2}{2-4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12 & =-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-10 & =0 \\\\ (k+2)(k-5) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=2,$ we need $k=-2$ or $k=5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1068 17592186511333 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1069 "q-140849" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1072 #uuid "4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511494 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1068 17592186511411 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1069 "q-95728" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1072 #uuid "524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511495 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1068 17592186511404 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1069 "q-36799" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1071 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1072 #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511496 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1068 17592186511446 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1069 "q-141619" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1071 "![](images/aa/aa70775eb1b2968406c61e1bebad52139f492890e71a9977582fc1898ba5ee8f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1072 #uuid "55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511497 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\n We see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-7\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1068 17592186511403 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1069 "q-78040" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1072 #uuid "5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511498 1074 "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nSince $x=1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $0.9$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-4.74$ | $≈-49.75$ | $≈-499.75$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1dbd1e97af1e0fc6a7776fba28f6c4bcb081fa3213291f66aeefc8b3219331e.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1068 17592186511339 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1069 "q-105621" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1071 "![](images/87/87bcea590bfb7357728d64cadabf4ab3e85135d312a60dd092ec546727a5fa30.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-1,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1072 #uuid "60c91108-e44c-5a52-8d9f-b008dcd257b3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511499 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-1)$ and $f(4)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-1)=5$ and $f(4)=-1$, we must have \n$k\\in [-1,5].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$-1$ and $5$\".\n\n![](images/1c/1c823d5f99ac9a126d3144197a493f347df20215c30b5ee7b28dc136459f00a2.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1068 17592186511428 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1069 "q-166362" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1071 "From the given data, the best forward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1072 #uuid "61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511500 1074 "First, we note that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{120-59}{1} \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a forward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $61$ customers per month." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1068 17592186511361 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1069 "q-346947" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1071 "Upon launching a new online course, the chief financial officer (CFO) of a company estimates that its profit, in thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=0.3{x}^{3}-3.6{x}^{2}+10.8x+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the price, in dollars, of a single course. If the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$ at what price is the maximum profit obtained?\n\nThe profit is maximum when a single course costs $$$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1072 #uuid "626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511501 1074 "Since $x$ represents the price of a single course and since the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$  the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 7.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<7.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8 & =0 \\\\ 0.9({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ 0.9(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=1.8x-7.2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =1.8(2)-7.2=-3.6<0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =1.8(6)-7.2=3.6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=2,$ the point $x=2$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2$ and $x=7.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =0.3(1)^{3}-3.6(1)^{2}+10.8(1)+5=12.5 \\\\ P(2) & =0.3(2)^{3}-3.6(2)^{2}+10.8(2)+5=14.6\\,✓ \\\\ P(7) & =0.3(7)^{3}-3.6(7)^{2}+10.8(7)+5=7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the profit is maximum when a single course costs $$\\,2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1068 17592186511423 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1069 "q-72402" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1071 "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})=$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1072 #uuid "62a31be5-1f88-5fce-bc3d-c7a604a6e638" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511502 1074 "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-{1}^{2})+({i}^{2}-{2}^{2})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-1)+({i}^{2}-4)) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5) & =(2(1)^{2}-5)+(2(2)^{2}-5)+(2(3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-3+3+13 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1068 17592186511356 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1069 "q-48342" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1072 #uuid "649e731a-c63d-5be5-bf6d-a7054ad65b37" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511503 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(4)=14$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,14].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $14$\"." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1068 17592186511321 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1069 "q-126568" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1072 #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511504 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1068 17592186511358 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1069 "q-166365" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1071 "Use your answer to part 3 to approximate the average number of customers at the campsite during the first $4$ months.\n\nThe average number of customers at the campsite over the first four months was\n{{field-1}}\n." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1072 #uuid "6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511505 1074 "The average value of a continuous function $q(t)$ on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈348.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}≈\\frac{348}{4-0}=87.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1068 17592186511354 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1069 "q-341244" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1071 "![](images/73/73610eebf575c612fc5b64ec6df264baffb338bdf3f2dbdc848477262874a54d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(-3)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1072 #uuid "6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511506 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4-7 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{5,9,2\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1068 17592186511391 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1069 "q-157227" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1072 #uuid "6d3ae80f-587f-5674-9679-5efb33bf0d2c" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511507 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1068 17592186511416 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1069 "q-340826" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k>0$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ {k}^{1-x}-10, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1072 #uuid "7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511508 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}(x-7) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}({k}^{1-x}-10) \\\\  & ={k}^{1-(-2)}-10 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-2)={k}^{3}-10$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {k}^{3}-10 & =-9 \\\\ {k}^{3} & =1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1068 17592186511386 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1069 "q-339855" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{x+3}, & x<2 \\\\ 2x+k, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1072 #uuid "74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511509 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{15}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2+3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+k) \\\\  & =2(2)+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2)=4+k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =3 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=2$ for the value $k=-1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1068 17592186511357 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1069 "q-344753" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-3,4].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-1$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$\n\nThe equation $g(x)=1$ has at least\n{{field-1}} solutions." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1072 #uuid "75621f90-4584-51d0-a7ef-873c52eae724" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511510 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-3,4],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-3,4].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-3,4]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-2],$ we have $g(-3)=-4$ and $g(-2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [-4,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,-1],$ we have $g(-2)=3$ and $g(-1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=0$ and $g(1)=-2.$ Since $1\\notin [-2,0],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,3],$ we have $g(1)=-2$ and $g(3)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=-5$ and $g(4)=-1.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-3,-2]$ and $[-2,-1].$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least $2$ solutions." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1068 17592186511432 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1069 "q-38218" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the value of $a,$ if any, for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1072 #uuid "791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511511 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-2}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}ax=2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ there is no value of $a$ that makes $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1068 17592186511418 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1069 "q-140961" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1072 #uuid "79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511512 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-1I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1068 17592186511369 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1069 "q-166363" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1071 "From the given data, the best backward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1072 #uuid "7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511513 1074 "Again, we use the fact that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1)-q(1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{59-0}{1} \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a backward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $59$ customers per month." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1068 17592186511334 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1069 "q-138536" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1072 #uuid "80fca04e-db43-5719-819a-d2aec209a00e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511514 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1068 17592186511370 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1069 "q-68052" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1072 #uuid "8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511515 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=1$ and $x=-5.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-5),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-5$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-5.1$ | $-5.01$ | $-5$ | $-4.99$ | $-4.9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-8.36$ | $-83.36$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $83.30$ | $8.30$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-5)$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-5)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and II is false.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-5$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41983c0d07e355a9bd12cf92af158c95f390a5785f4bbc1d08562c240f190623.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1068 17592186511429 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1069 "q-142517" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1071 "![](images/72/72c697336f9518e5be1c8b89d9244c0952a7b8f5e77f007638e6a9405d98d8fd.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠ODC.$\n\n$m∠ODC=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1072 #uuid "839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511516 1074 "Since $\\overset{―}{OC}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are radii of the circle, the triangle $△OCD$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\n∠OCD=∠ODC.\n$$\n\nAlso, from step 3, we know that $m∠COD={(180-10x)}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/50/509c6fd7ffabd5c4f7b71e89dd94cb29c374dd6d2d64407d65bcdef44553c60e.png)\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC+m∠OCD+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC+(m∠ODC)+(180-10x)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC-10{x}^{∘} & ={0}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC & =10{x}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC & =5{x}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1068 17592186511368 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1069 "q-119028" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1071 "A school is organizing a trip to a theme park. The trip is offered to at least $12$ kids, and it can be attended by at most $20$ kids. The cost of each ticket is $$80$ if there are exactly $12$ kids. If there are more than $12$ kids, the price per ticket drops by $$4$ for each kid in excess of $12.$ Every kid pays the same amount for going on the trip.\n\nHow many kids must go on the trip for the theme park to maximize its revenue?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1072 #uuid "83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511517 1074 "First, note that if there are $12+x$ kids, then the price per ticket is $80-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of kids}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(80-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per ticket}} \\\\  & =(12+x)(80-4x) \\\\  & =960-48x+80x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =960+32x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there can only be between $12$ and $20$ kids, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\le x+12\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=8$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=32-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=4$ lies in the interval $0<x<8.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=4$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=4$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the theme park should admit $12+4=16$ kids to maximize its revenue." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1068 17592186511415 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1069 "q-48425" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,8].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,8]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1072 #uuid "889fc732-d136-5234-a89b-7831dcd5eacb" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511518 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,8],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(8).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(8)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $2$\"." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1068 17592186511326 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1069 "q-48431" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ k, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1072 #uuid "89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511519 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-1)}{k(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{k\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+3}{k}=\\frac{4}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $k=\\pm 2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1068 17592186511323 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1069 "q-347242" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1071 "A plant spends $C(x)=150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousands of dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product per month. The plant sells the product for $$200\\,000$ per unit. Given that the plant can produce between $2$ and $9$ units per month, how many units should the plant produce per month to maximize its profit?\n\nThe optimal number of plant production is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1072 #uuid "896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511520 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product, the plant has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ thousands of dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+180x-150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $2\\le x\\le 9.$ Therefore, $x=2$ and $x=9$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $2<x<9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-48x+180,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-48x+180 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-16x+60 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =6,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the critical points $x=6$ and $x=10,$ only $x=6$ is within the permitted domain.\n\nAll that's left now is to check if $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =6x-48 \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-12<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at $x=6$ is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a maximum.\n\nOur candidates are $x=2,x=6$ and $x=9.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =(2)^{3}-24(2)^{2}+180(2)-150=122 \\\\ P(6) & =(6)^{3}-24(6)^{2}+180(6)-150=282\\,✓ \\\\ P(9) & =(9)^{3}-24(9)^{2}+180(9)-150=255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the plant will have a maximum profit level when it produces $6$ units per month." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1068 17592186511359 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1068 17592186511383 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1068 17592186511390 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1069 "q-215488" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\frac{3}{5}+|\\frac{1-3x}{5}|<2.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1072 #uuid "8ba584f9-4667-5d0f-bd62-9fcff937c1e1" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511521 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}+|\\frac{1-3x}{5}| & <2 \\\\ |\\frac{1-3x}{5}| & <\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{1-3x}{5}$ whose distance from zero is less than $\\frac{7}{5}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{5}<\\frac{1-3x}{5}<\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{5}<\\frac{1-3x}{5} & <\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -7<1-3x & <7 \\\\ -8<-3x & <6 \\\\ 8>3x & >-6 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}>x & >-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n-2<x<\\frac{8}{3}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1068 17592186511322 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1069 "q-119098" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1071 "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1072 #uuid "8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511522 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(5-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(3+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1068 17592186511425 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1069 "q-47084" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-1)(x+1)(x-2).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[0,3]?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1072 #uuid "8c198801-a87e-5360-90a9-0643872362f6" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511523 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,3],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(3).$\n\nSince $f(0)=2$ and $f(3)=8,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=0$ does not belong to the interval $[2,8].$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1068 17592186511338 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1068 17592186511396 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1069 "q-340800" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1072 #uuid "8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511524 1074 "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1068 17592186511438 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1068 17592186511441 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1069 "q-303064" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=k?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2}, & x<k \\\\ -2k-x, & x\\ge k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,k=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1072 #uuid "8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511525 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+4) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}(-2k-x) \\\\  & =-2k-k \\\\  & =-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(k)=-3k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}+2k+4 & =-3k \\\\ {k}^{2}+5k+4 & =0 \\\\ (k+4)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-4,\\,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=k$ for the values\n\n$$\nk=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,k=-1.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1068 17592186511401 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1069 "q-103926" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4x+8y.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1072 #uuid "8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511526 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1068 17592186511437 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1069 "q-152896" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1072 #uuid "8da6073f-aed1-5284-a635-95231dda9375" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511527 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1068 17592186511374 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1069 "q-343853" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1071 "![](images/b1/b1d5208f46af834deda70086b9ef4b91defe7359f625d9fb264b370965196a0b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1072 #uuid "8f946771-a506-530c-b84d-03471b2aa334" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511528 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-4,3],$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ae/aea529468efff36403aca0bf9ee9cc6bc4c5680476ccb81314388bb315f700df.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5+(-1.5)+(-4) \\\\  & =-3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,2,-3.5\\}=-3.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1068 17592186511440 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1069 "q-15744" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1071 "![](images/39/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1072 #uuid "907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511529 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/82/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1068 17592186511343 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1069 "q-48328" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k}, & x\\ne k \\\\ -4, & x=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=k$, find the value of the constant $k.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1072 #uuid "99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511530 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k} & =\\frac{(x+k)(x-k)}{(x-k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+k)\\cancel{(x-k)}}{\\cancel{x-k}} \\\\  & =x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=k,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to k}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to k}(x+k)=k+k=2k.\n$$\n\nFor $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(k) & ={lim}_{x\\to k}h(x) \\\\ -4 & =2k \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=k,$ we need $k=-2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1068 17592186511436 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1069 "q-142504" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1071 "![](images/f3/f3f38ca1b55a12dcaffc5a52eb4f08fc6d6ec3d5c746cb8215ad3e0ed7671153.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠COD.$\n\n$m∠COD=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1072 #uuid "99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511531 1074 "We're given that $∠BOE$ is a straight angle, so\n\n$$\nm∠BOE={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOE & =m∠BOE \\\\ (5x+5)^{∘}+m∠COD+(5x-5)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10{x}^{∘}+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠COD & =(180-10x)^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1068 17592186511317 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1068 17592186511328 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1068 17592186511381 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1068 17592186511413 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1069 "q-343448" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1072 #uuid "9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511532 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1068 17592186511421 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1069 "q-144837" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1071 "![](images/66/6684670c5999281044dd1e08faabbf06e7aeb3fef7902835ea9ea6c07d148b5a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $f(-2)=-3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1072 #uuid "9b464b81-5baf-5338-aa06-b3e5f256249d" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511533 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+8 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,5\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1068 17592186511397 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1069 "q-47053" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,8].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=-2?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1072 #uuid "9d898f3e-75a2-51d9-91a1-d58badf3f268" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511534 1074 "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[-1,8]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,8].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,8]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $f(-1)=-5$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-2\\in [-5,0],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,2],$ we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=4.$ Since $-2\\notin [0,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,4],$ we have $f(2)=4$ and $f(4)=-1.$ Since $-2\\notin [-1,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $f(4)=-1$ and $f(5)=-4.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,-1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,8],$ we have $f(5)=-4$ and $f(8)=8.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,8],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0],$ $[4,5],$ and $[5,8].$ Therefore, $f(x)=-2$ has at least three solutions." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1068 17592186511320 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1069 "q-166366" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1071 "For $0\\le t\\le 8,$ the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least{{field-1}}\nsolution(s)." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1072 #uuid "9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511535 1074 "The solutions to ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ correspond to the stationary points of the graph of $q(t).$\n\nSketching a possible graph of $q(t),$ we get the following:\n\n![](images/2f/2fd9e08520f4a671a255f360e583140175650de856a12233962c99a064c4c582.png)\n\nFrom our sketch, we notice three stationary points located near $t=2,$ $t=3,$ and $t=4.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least three solutions." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1068 17592186511331 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1069 "q-346992" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1071 "A photography studio books $x$ photo sessions per day at a price per session $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the studio books between $1$ and $6$ sessions per day, how many sessions should it book to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize revenue, the studio should book\n{{field-1}}\nphoto sessions per day." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1072 #uuid "a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511536 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x+45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-18x+15,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ However, $x=1$ is already a candidate, so the new candidate is $x=5.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,\\,5,$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-9(1)^{2}+15(1)+45=1-9+15+45=52\\,✓ \\\\ R(5) & =(5)^{3}-9(5)^{2}+15(5)+45=125-225+75+45=20 \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-9(6)^{2}+15(6)+45=216-324+90+45=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize revenue, the studio should book $1$ photo session per day." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1068 17592186511332 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1069 "q-343959" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1071 "![](images/3c/3cb97b20d164c2976ae046f83edbbb499514ff3c7ddfbfa9b703918457eba467.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,6]$ given that $f(-4)=7.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1072 #uuid "a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511537 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$  Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of  $f(x)$  for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/e6/e67e51128fe4392e814eec6d9d6ab17d7d7718af38fd556a0a47c603174e9dcd.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =7, \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+2 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24, \\\\ f(6) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =24-4.5 \\\\  & =19.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{7,9,24,19.5\\}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=24,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1068 17592186511410 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1069 "q-347264" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1071 "A factory spends  $C(x)=0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000$ dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $30-\\frac{x}{100}$ dollars per unit. Given that the factory can produce at most $2\\,000$ units per year, how many units should the factory produce per year to maximize its annual profit?\n\nThe optimal number of production is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1072 #uuid "a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511538 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per year, the factory has an annual revenue (income) of $R(x)=(30-\\frac{x}{100})\\cdot x=30x-0.01{x}^{2}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =30x-0.01{x}^{2}-(0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000) \\\\  & =-1\\,000+29.7x-0.012375{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 2\\,000.$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<2\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=29.7-0.02475x.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 29.7-0.02475x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1\\,200$ lies in the interval $0<x<2\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1\\,200$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:   \n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02475<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1\\,200$ is indeed a  maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=1\\,200,$ and $x=2\\,000.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1\\,000 \\\\ P(1\\,200) & =-1\\,000+29.7(1\\,200)-0.012375(1\\,200)^{2}=16\\,820\\,✓ \\\\ P(2\\,000) & =-1\\,000+29.7(2\\,000)-0.012375(2\\,000)^{2}=9\\,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the factory will have a maximum annual profit when it produces $1\\,200$ units per year." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1068 17592186511348 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1069 "q-149498" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [5,10],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (5,10).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[5,10]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum overestimates the true area." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1072 #uuid "a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511539 1074 "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(5,10),$ we have \n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{3}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{3{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1068 17592186511340 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1069 "q-39987" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1071 "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1072 #uuid "a905591c-e962-54f6-a8cc-c6c8bf83d953" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511540 1074 "Since $u⊥v$, they are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-5)+(-3)\\cdot 1+2k\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot k \\\\  & =-5-3+8k-2k \\\\  & =6k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-8 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1068 17592186511393 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1069 "q-123643" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1072 #uuid "acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511541 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1068 17592186511314 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1069 "q-126754" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1071 "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1072 #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511542 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1068 17592186511435 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1069 "q-166364" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1071 "Using a trapezoidal sum with two equal subintervals, ${\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈${{field-1}}\n." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1072 #uuid "ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511543 1074 "The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(a)+2(q({t}_{1})+q({t}_{2})+…+q({t}_{n-1}))+q(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n* The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n* The corresponding interval is $[0,4],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=4.$\n\n* The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-0}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(0)+2q(2)+q(4)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(120)+108]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240+108 \\\\  & =348.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1068 17592186511352 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1069 "q-143298" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}∼N(2,{6}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $18$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\n*Hint: Value tables of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3192\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0197\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0188\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1072 #uuid "af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511544 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{{6}^{2}}{18}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =P(-1.05<\\overset{―}{X}<1.05) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{-1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-2.16<Z<-0.67) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.67) & =0.2514, \\\\ Φ(-2.16) & =0.0154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16) \\\\  & =0.2514-0.0154 \\\\  & =0.2360.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1068 17592186511433 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1069 "q-81033" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(1,\\pi )$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1072 #uuid "b2d69fc5-a88d-5940-a2a9-d7cc001b13e7" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511545 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,\\pi ).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,\\pi )=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1068 17592186511417 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1069 "q-123634" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1072 #uuid "b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511546 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1068 17592186511360 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1069 "q-161923" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1071 "![](images/98/989c9452bebc4ee6c2253c920655830a0b3d0adf0ddb7b90c1ea6a9528ef1448.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ for $y\\le 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1072 #uuid "b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511547 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/df8a9da3b88f3b40d85005b156215f7fa7760257f284acf98ec186d5a2e3ab8d.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function  $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1068 17592186511427 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1069 "q-346953" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1071 "The cost for a company to produce $x$ bicycles, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nC(x)={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+300.\n$$\n\nGiven that the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, how many bicycles should the company produce per week to minimize its weekly cost?\n\nThe company should produce\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles per week to minimize its weekly cost." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1072 #uuid "bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511548 1074 "Let $x$ represent the number of bicycles produced per week. Since the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 10.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $C(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+48,\n$$\n\nand solving ${C}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-10x+16) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)(x-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=8$ gives a minimum value of $C(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6x-30\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=8$ into ${C}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{″}(2) & =12-30=-18<0, \\\\ {C}^{″}(8) & =48-30=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive at $x=8,$ the point $x=8$ is a local minimum of $C(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=8$ and $x=10.$ Computing $C(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(0) & =300 \\\\ C(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+300=236\\,✓ \\\\ C(10) & =(10)^{3}-15(10)^{2}+48(10)+300=280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is the global minimum of $C(x),$ and the company should produce $8$ bicycles per week to minimize weekly cost." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1068 17592186511395 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1069 "q-36677" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1071 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1072 #uuid "c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511549 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1068 17592186511345 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1068 17592186511422 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1069 "q-142519" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1071 "![](images/82/828ac748b9d8c87e4a7a66dfddb2e926a3362830d551653260b5974b6bd2ca1a.png)\n\nGiven that the line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and $\\overset{―}{AF}$ are parallel and $m∠FAO={(3x+18)}^{∘},$ find the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠FAO=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1072 #uuid "c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511550 1074 "The parallel line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}∥\\overset{―}{AF}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{―}{AD}.$\n\nThe angles $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ are interior to the parallel line segments and are on opposite sides of the transversal. So, they are alternate interior angles and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\nIn step 4, we found that $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$\n\nSetting the measures of $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ equal to each other, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC & =m∠FAO \\\\ 5{x}^{∘} & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\ 5x & =3x+18 \\\\ 2x & =18 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=9$ into the expressions for the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FAO & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 9+18)^{∘} \\\\  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOE & =(185-5x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(185-5\\cdot 9)^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠FAO={45}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1068 17592186511313 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1069 "q-142509" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1071 "If $AD=8,$ then $OC=${{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1072 #uuid "c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511551 1074 "Notice that the points $A,C,$ and $D$ lie on the circle. We know that the radius of the circle is $r=OC.$\n\nWe first notice that\n\n$$\nAD=AO+OD.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because $AD=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =8 \\\\ AO+OD & =8 \\\\ r+r & =8 \\\\ 2r & =8 \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nOC=r=4.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1068 17592186511434 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1069 "q-142529" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1071 "![](images/96/96a76584c5ddbcc39d221334803c6791cb3b459178e70c310e4eca54a4e85462.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of the angle $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1072 #uuid "c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511552 1074 "Since $∠AOD$ is a straight angle, the angles $∠AOE$ and $∠DOE$ are supplementary. Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the measure of the angle $∠AOE$ that's supplementary to the angle of $∠DOE=5x-{5}^{∘}$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOE & ={180}^{∘}-(5x-5)^{∘} \\\\  & =({180}^{∘}+{5}^{∘})-5{x}^{∘} \\\\  & ={(185-5x)}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1068 17592186511444 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1069 "q-343902" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1071 "![](images/86/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1072 #uuid "c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511553 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1068 17592186511377 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1069 "q-15324" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1071 "A fast-food chain wants to open some new restaurants. The company estimates that its annual profit, in hundreds of thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-12x+20,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of new restaurants it opens up. If the company has a budget to open up between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, how many restaurants should the company open to maximize its profit?" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1072 #uuid "c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511554 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of  new restaurants opened, and since the company can open between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 8.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-{x}^{2}+8x-12.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-2x+8\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =-2(2)+8=4>0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-2(6)+8=-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=6,$ the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=6$ and $x=8.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+4(1)^{2}-12(1)+20=\\frac{35}{3}≈12 \\\\ P(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}+4(6)^{2}-12(6)+20=20\\,✓ \\\\ P(8) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{3}+4(8)^{2}-12(8)+20=\\frac{28}{3}≈9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the company must open $6$ restaurants to maximize its profit." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1068 17592186511325 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1068 17592186511398 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1069 "q-340808" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1072 #uuid "cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511555 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1068 17592186511430 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1069 "q-144853" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1071 "![](images/ff/ffa54937d4977e1b2e815862ad20fa7c05338fa4015f6ba42c5617c8957ea45d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,4]$ given that $f(-3)=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1072 #uuid "ce30ca10-ec44-5f96-b53c-efeee52b65e4" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511556 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-6 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1068 17592186511344 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1069 "q-342845" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1071 "![](images/d9/d9a2a079f471072c80becb1188a2862f6f5d0d699eb40989b01c27a1f079fc42.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-10,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-10,6]$ given that $f(-10)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1072 #uuid "d62efc40-a923-5983-a507-878080289da7" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511557 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-10,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-10,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-10,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-10) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(-10)+{\\int }_{-10}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,11\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-10.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1068 17592186511424 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1069 "q-19084" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1071 "![](images/2a/2aea7b223ec18094d626e8d8937a5844e786cb1b936fb3b6b478f672e4d0fb9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1072 #uuid "d754760c-74db-51d7-aeca-199ef191761d" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511558 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of one triangle and one trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d8/d811c4920b4ddb05170afc1f0c38fe216c23139556b6f7e10829b663e894a7fb.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1.5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5)+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1.5,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1068 17592186511312 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1069 "q-128641" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1071 "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬P∧Q)∨(R∧Q)?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1072 #uuid "d785d990-0971-50ad-9ff0-c1525a2beb2a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511559 1074 "We can factor out the conjunction with $Q,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬P∧Q)∨(R∧Q)≡(¬P∨R)∧Q\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1068 17592186511448 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1069 "q-161749" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1072 #uuid "d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511560 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1068 17592186511402 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1069 "q-15776" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1071 "![](images/52/527c042c57a965b22f4e9841e59087291382704d6ea29f5f444926257444d5f6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,4]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1072 #uuid "d9b31f47-0173-5340-9051-1c0bbc0f3595" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511561 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/f5/f512273895da627c314f00a55bd507d6ae43cd1c6af95d98bd4b475903303e7d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3+(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,1,4,3\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1068 17592186511371 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1069 "q-142717" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1071 "![](images/4b/4ba93d3eb8944d086b001c0aa01cb28251705a6af9e55a9fd58e2affce12acaa.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $AOEF,$ the angle $∠E$ measures ${65}^{∘}.$ Then, $m∠F=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1072 #uuid "db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511562 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides, so the sum of its interior angles must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom step 5, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠FAO={45}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠O & =m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f6/f67441e3999a8b81e7ac63555c729f0f6c31bff015477efc3031364c6fb22003.png)\n\nAdding up the interior angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠O+m∠E+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {45}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{65}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {250}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠F & ={110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠F={110}^{∘}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1068 17592186511408 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1069 "q-149499" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [2,6],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (2,6).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6]$ using a right Riemann sum.  Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1072 #uuid "dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511563 1074 "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(2,6),$ we have \n\n$$\n2x>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{2}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{2x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{2x\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1068 17592186511353 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1069 "q-140491" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1071 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1072 #uuid "dcb73b7c-8cd5-5969-93ed-1bf898ab0795" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511564 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{17}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1068 17592186511365 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1069 "q-316990" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1071 "![](images/d4/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1072 #uuid "de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511565 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/aa/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1068 17592186511382 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1069 "q-347261" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1071 "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1072 #uuid "de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511566 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1068 17592186511350 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1068 17592186511419 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1068 17592186511449 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1069 "q-212474" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|3x+2|<7.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1072 #uuid "dfe6edfc-d27b-50ff-aa28-a96fc85e1d12" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511567 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $3x+2$ whose distance from zero is less than $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7<3x+2<7\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7<3x+2 & <7 \\\\ -9<3x & <5 \\\\ -3<x & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1068 17592186511311 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1069 "q-138617" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1072 #uuid "e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511568 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511569 1068 17592186511392 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511569 1069 "q-144833" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511569 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511569 1071 "![](images/56/567044329a721ac622d2f66e675577e202d65d3de145a93fa430edfb31ced4b1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown 