{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nSince $x=-1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $-1.1$ | $-1.01$ | $-1.001$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-23.21$ | $≈-203.02$ | $≈-2\\,003.00$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2967a50cc89632da9292fc9192232f757ff3f3bec9304042ef6ec074882565b5.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1068 17592186511366 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1069 "q-15774" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1071 "![](images/80/807e4e931a2872dd6d2a570547b75f68935b3ea761cfaa8cda279a0e194130f9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=-10.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1072 #uuid "e74c85cc-5f64-54fa-a3ca-fa87e47bcbd2" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511571 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3-5 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-10,-7,-12\\}=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-12,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1068 17592186511363 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1069 "q-144856" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1071 "![](images/78/78d04b9ba0d4ad4e84ec45e88a199af6dbbdb04b1fa9d7513f23d9eb64a99a47.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $4.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,0]$ given that $f(-4)=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1072 #uuid "e788b4b9-a09c-5261-9e6a-52c7021cb74e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511572 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1068 17592186511450 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1069 "q-29942" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|4z+2|>6.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1072 #uuid "e891fb42-cafd-57b7-9773-6b5a4bb29926" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511573 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $4z+2$ whose distance from zero is greater than $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 & \\text{or} &  & 4z+2 & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 &  &  & 4z+2 & >6 \\\\ 4z & <-8 &  &  & 4z & >4 \\\\ z & <-2 &  &  & z & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & <-2 & \\text{or} &  & z & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1068 17592186511330 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1069 "q-146236" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|6}=\\frac{15}{2}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1072 #uuid "e9ff9199-1184-598e-a945-b789f3d5b0b6" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511574 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ | $\\frac{23}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{20}{23}$ | $\\frac{3}{23}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,6)]-{\\mu }_{Y|6}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+50-\\frac{225}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1068 17592186511414 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1069 "q-316188" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1071 "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1072 #uuid "eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511575 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ ride passes per day, the park has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=15x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the park is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{700}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =15x-(700+2x) \\\\  & =13x-700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=500$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=13,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=500.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =13(0)-700=-700, \\\\ P(500) & =13(500)-700=6500-700=5800\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of ride passes that maximizes the park’s profit is $500.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1068 17592186511315 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1069 "q-340784" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1072 #uuid "f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511576 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{5}.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1068 17592186511378 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1069 "q-141882" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1071 "![](images/82/82a08c804381b11ae118691d82c4801b655cd8e0b131677f67ed6e63b8400d7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[2,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[2,6]$ given that $f(2)=-2.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1072 #uuid "f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511577 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1068 17592186511327 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1069 "q-149437" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1070 17592186045485 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1071 "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,12].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1072 #uuid "f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511578 1074 "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{12-0}{3}=4.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $18$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (18+0+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =104\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1068 17592186511409 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1069 "q-346317" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1071 "The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[-4,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length $L$ of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1072 #uuid "f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511579 1074 "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-4)}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $8$ | $4$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(8+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1068 17592186511447 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1069 "q-215469" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2}\\le 1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1072 #uuid "f658914c-7028-577a-9b28-3f8336734446" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511580 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2} & \\le 1 \\\\ |\\frac{2x+1}{4}| & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{2x+1}{4}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $\\frac{5}{2}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\text{ }-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4}\\le \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10\\le 2x+1 & \\le 10 \\\\ -11\\le 2x & \\le 9 \\\\ -\\frac{11}{2}\\le x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1068 17592186511362 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1069 "q-107245" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1072 #uuid "f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511581 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-2),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-2$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-2.01$ | $-2.001$ | $-2$ | $-1.999$ | $-1.99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈40.12$ | $≈400.12$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $≈-399.88$ | $≈-39.88$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-2)$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-2)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is false and II is true.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is also true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25e672e71c4268193c128687ba35eeca1e4bce8f0c355922a3b5df022740159c.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1068 17592186511318 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1069 "q-146282" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1070 17592186045486 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=1.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1072 #uuid "fa585366-16bd-5221-b5b1-a80000ac4783" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511582 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}]-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+1-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1068 17592186511341 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1069 "q-48358" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1071 "![](images/de/dededc3b9924ff278f45a6149a0b7a5ca0464ee2c3b80aa85d12187a86a93725.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $-1$ and $1$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1072 #uuid "fc22bd7d-9c0b-50f8-80e8-b40ec1e8460f" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511583 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(3)=-1.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,3]$\"\n\n![](images/85/85a590ba283dae96329692fee1aa6e08045fb06e5baa8a42d8308fe8c2558f02.png)" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1068 17592186511400 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1069 "q-42297" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1071 "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1072 #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511584 1074 "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1068 17592186511367 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1069 "q-140124" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1070 17592186045487 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ k \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -k-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1072 #uuid "fff3565b-721f-5145-afe3-b8fe4b216b1a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511585 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 3+k\\cdot (-k-1)+k\\cdot (-7)+3\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =21-{k}^{2}-k-7k-36 \\\\  & =-{k}^{2}-8k-15 \\\\  & =-({k}^{2}+8k+15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}+8k+15) & =0 \\\\ -(k+5)(k+3) & =0 \\\\ k & =-5,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511586 1058 17592186511666 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511586 1062 #uuid "0300a9fc-85df-55bb-885b-ea046fb4bb5e" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511586 1063 17592186511456 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511587 1058 17592186511598 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511587 1062 #uuid "05862192-baca-5966-ba59-871832f53fa7" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511587 1063 17592186402302 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511588 1058 17592186511642 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511588 1062 #uuid "0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511588 1063 17592186511539 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511589 1058 17592186511648 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511589 1062 #uuid "0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186511589 1063 17592186511457 13194139535523 17592186045417 true] [17592186